.TH SDL_GetPrefPath 3 "SDL 3.5.0" "Simple Directmedia Layer" "SDL3 FUNCTIONS"
.SH NAME
SDL_GetPrefPath \- Get the user-and-app-specific path where files can be written.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <SDL3/SDL_filesystem.h>
.PP
.BI "char * SDL_GetPrefPath(const char *org, const char *app);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Get the \(dqpref dir\(dq\[char46] This is meant to be where users can write personal files (preferences and save games, etc) that are specific to your application\[char46] This directory is unique per user, per application\[char46]
.PP
This function will decide the appropriate location in the native filesystem, create the directory if necessary, and return a string of the absolute path to the directory in UTF-8 encoding\[char46]
.PP
On Windows, the string might look like:
.PP
.BR "C:\(rs\(rsUsers\(rs\(rsbob\(rs\(rsAppData\(rs\(rsRoaming\(rs\(rsMy Company\(rs\(rsMy Program Name\(rs\(rs"
.PP
On Linux, the string might look like:
.PP
.BR "/home/bob/\[char46]local/share/My Program Name/"
.PP
On macOS, the string might look like:
.PP
.BR "/Users/bob/Library/Application Support/My Program Name/"
.PP
You should assume the path returned by this function is the only safe place to write files (and that
.BR SDL_GetBasePath (),
while it might be writable, or even the parent of the returned path, isn\(aqt where you should be writing things)\[char46]
.PP
Both the org and app strings may become part of a directory name, so please follow these rules:
.PP

\(bu Try to use the same org string (_including case-sensitivity_) for all your applications that use this function\[char46]
.PP
\(bu Always use a unique app string for each one, and make sure it never changes for an app once you\(aqve decided on it\[char46]
.PP
\(bu Unicode characters are legal, as long as they are UTF-8 encoded, but\[char46]\[char46]\[char46]
.PP
\(bu \[char46]\[char46]\[char46]only use letters, numbers, and spaces\[char46] Avoid punctuation like \(dqGame Name 2: Bad Guy\(aqs Revenge!\(dq \[char46]\[char46]\[char46] \(dqGame Name 2\(dq is sufficient\[char46]
.PP
Due to historical mistakes,
.BR org
is allowed to be NULL or \(dq\(dq\[char46] In such cases, SDL will omit the org subdirectory, including on platforms where it shouldn\(aqt, and including on platforms where this would make your app fail certification for an app store\[char46] New apps should definitely specify a real string for
.BR org \[char46]
.PP
The returned path is guaranteed to end with a path separator (\(aq\(rs\(rs\(aq on Windows, \(aq/\(aq on most other platforms)\[char46]

.SH FUNCTION PARAMETERS
.TP
.I org
the name of your organization\[char46]
.TP
.I app
the name of your application\[char46]
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns a UTF-8 string of the user directory in platform-dependent notation\[char46] NULL if there\(aqs a problem (creating directory failed, etc\[char46])\[char46] This should be freed with
.BR SDL_free ()
when it is no longer needed\[char46]

.SH THREAD SAFETY
It is safe to call this function from any thread\[char46]

.SH AVAILABILITY
This function is available since SDL 3\[char46]2\[char46]0\[char46]

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR SDL_GetBasePath (3)
